Command:
Get-Volume | Select-Object DriveLetter, @{N="FreeGB";E={[math]::Round($_.SizeRemaining/1GB,2)}}

Response:
DriveLetter FreeGB
----------- ------
          C 234.03
              0.11

Explanation: The prompt obtains data about disk letter (C, ..., used for order), remaining memory space from OS and it gets displayed in columns. Get-Volume reaches the OS (Windows in this case), interrogates about the information, regarding drives' letters (C in this case with the first),
and remaining volume of memory (measured in GB that stands for gigabytes and showing 234.03, which was rounded on purpose not to overload the column),
which it retrieves and displays in columns. Select-Object filters the data to relate only to drives, which then labels remaining amount of memory space
as 'FreeGB' and sets calculations, that involve division. 1GB is written to turn huge string (amount of bytes) into understandable gigabytes. Second 
drive has no letter labeled as disk letter (because it's service partition that is not displayed in explorer) and remaining space of 0.11 GB. 




Command:
Get-Disk | Select-Object Number, FriendlyName

Response:
Number FriendlyName
------ ------------
     0 KBG60ZNV512G KIOXIA

Explanation: The prompt obtains data about disk model number and it's name. Get-Disk returns disk's model number (this is what column 'Number'
is about) that is 0 (numerical order), as well as model name (the column with 'Friendly Name' label that means the name assigned to model) - which consists of 
model code (KBG60ZNV512G) and producer name (KIOXIA), that results in a full identifier - KBG60ZNV512G KIOXIA.



Command:
Get-PhysicalDisk | Select-Object DeviceId, MediaType

Response:
DeviceId MediaType
-------- ---------
0        SSD

Explanation:
Here, the disk ID (in column labeled as 'DeviceId', that means) and importantly, disk type (which is meant as 'MediaType') are retrieved
by command Get-PhysicalDisk, which find data about physical parameters of disk. The DeviceId (disk ID) is 0 (disk order), while MediaType 
(disk type) is SSD, which means solid-state drive, modern storage device.




Command:
Get-Disk | Select-Object FriendlyName, BusType

Response:
FriendlyName        BusType
------------        -------
KBG60ZNV512G KIOXIA NVMe


Explanation:
The prompt resulted in display of disk name (FriendlyName - KBG60ZNV512G KIOXIA) and physical interface protocol (BusType), through which disk exchanges data
from motherboard, in this case, displayed as NVMe, that means ultra-fast protocol for SSD on PCIe lanes.









